{
  "an4.163:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 4.163 ",
  "an4.163:0.2": "17. Practice ",
  "an4.163:0.3": "Ugly ",
  "an4.163:1.1": "“Mendicants, there are four ways of practice. ",
  "an4.163:1.2": "What four? ",
  "an4.163:1.3": "Painful practice with slow insight, ",
  "an4.163:1.4": "painful practice with swift insight, ",
  "an4.163:1.5": "pleasant practice with slow insight, and ",
  "an4.163:1.6": "pleasant practice with swift insight. ",
  "an4.163:2.1": "And what’s the painful practice with slow insight? ",
  "an4.163:2.2": "It’s when a mendicant meditates observing the ugliness of the body, perceives the repulsiveness of food, perceives dissatisfaction with the whole world, observes the impermanence of all conditions, ",
  "an4.163:2.3": "and has well established the perception of their own death. ",
  "an4.163:2.4": "They rely on these five powers of a trainee: ",
  "an4.163:2.5": "faith, conscience, prudence, energy, and wisdom. ",
  "an4.163:2.6": "But these five faculties manifest in them weakly: ",
  "an4.163:2.7": "faith, energy, mindfulness, immersion, and wisdom. ",
  "an4.163:2.8": "Because of this, they only slowly attain the conditions for ending the defilements in the present life. ",
  "an4.163:2.9": "This is called the painful practice with slow insight. ",
  "an4.163:3.1": "And what’s the painful practice with swift insight? ",
  "an4.163:3.2": "It’s when a mendicant meditates observing the ugliness of the body, perceives the repulsiveness of food, perceives dissatisfaction with the